            <ArticleTitle>Influence of orally administered Lactobacillus GG on respiratory immune response in a murine model of diet-induced obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>99-103</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1348-0421.12226</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mice with diet-induced obesity were fed with Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG (LGG) suspended in saline or saline alone (control mice). Pulmonary mRNA expression of IFN-γ; IFN-α receptor 1; CD247 antigen; killer cell lectin-like receptor subfamily K, member 1; TNF-α; IL-12 receptor β1 and IL-2 receptor β, and the proportion of Lactobacillales in feces were significantly greater in the LGG group than in the control mice (P &lt; 0.05 and P &lt; 0.01, respectively). These results suggest that LGG alters the respiratory immunity of obese subjects through having a potent impact on intestinal immunity.</AbstractText>
